diff --git a/docs/spec/nxdn-trunking.md b/docs/spec/nxdn-trunking.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..437b087
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/spec/nxdn-trunking.md
@@ -0,0 +1,89 @@
+<!-- SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-3.0-or-later -->
+<!-- Copyright (c) 2026 lyra XE1POC -->
+
+# NXDN Type-C trunking
+
+An NXDN channel (or the NXDN VFO) marked trunked camps on a Type-C site's control channel, reads its CAC, follows
+the voice channel assignments it wants and comes back when the call's voice stops. Where the channel may transmit it
+also registers with the site and, on PTT, asks for a voice channel and transmits on the one assigned. Both rates
+(NXDN48 at 4800 bit/s, NXDN96 at 9600 bit/s) work the same way: the rate is the channel's and is used on the control
+channel and the voice channels alike.
+
+The firmware is `core/nxdn/nxdn_trunk.c` (the follower), `core/nxdn/nxdn_frame.c` (CAC and long CAC),
+`nxdn_rx.c` (control channel frames), `nxdn_call.c` and `nxdn_engine.c` (the inbound burst, RTCH on a traffic
+channel), `core/radio/radio_nxdn.c` (the radio side) and `core/radio/radio_sm.c` (PTT, the grant and the bursts, shared
+with P25 trunking). Section numbers are NXDN TS 1-A's.
+
+Status: host tests only (`tests/host/nxdnTest.c` testCac and testLongCac, `tests/host/nxdnTrunkTest.c`,
+`tests/host/uiFlowTest.c` "NXDN trunk"). **Not verified against a real Type-C site.** The coding of the CAC (CRC-16,
+puncturing, interleaving) agrees with DSD-FME's decoder bit for bit (its CRC check and puncture table); the long CAC,
+the inbound LICH and the message layouts follow SDRTrunk's decoder. Nothing here has been checked against a site's
+own receiver: test first on a system you are authorized to use.
+
+## 1. What it does
+
+| Item | Behaviour |
+|---|---|
+| Control channel | The channel's RX frequency; its TX frequency is the control channel's inbound one. One per channel |
+| Frames taken | LICH RF = RCCH, USC = CAC, direction outbound. RDCH/RTCH as before |
+| CAC (outbound) | 155 bits (SR 8, message 144, 3 spare), CRC-16 x16+x12+x5+1 (register loaded with ones), 4 tail bits, K = 5 code, of each 14 coded bits the 4th and 12th erased (300 bits), 25 x 12 interleaver, behind the LICH |
+| Long CAC (inbound) | 136 bits (SR 8, message 128), the same CRC-16, 4 tail bits, of each 26 coded bits the 2nd, 8th, 10th, 12th and 20th erased (252 bits), 21 x 12 interleaver. LICH 0x08 (RCCH, long CAC, inbound). A burst is the preamble, FSW, LICH and the long CAC (144 symbols), sent as soon as the radio can |
+| RAN | The channel's RX RAN filters the CAC's SR (0: any); bursts carry the TX RAN |
+| Messages read | SITE_INFO (0x18): location ID and channel access information. VCALL_ASSGN (0x04), VCALL_ASSGN_DUP (0x05). REG_RESP (0x20). VCALL_RESP (0x01) |
+| Messages sent | REG_REQ (0x20): the location ID's first 19 bits, our unit, our group. VCALL_REQ (0x01): group or individual call, 4800 or 9600 EHR, our unit, the talk target |
+| Followed | Group calls to the channel's group, calls to all (65535), individual calls to our unit ID; with "every group" or digital monitor, every group call. While a voice request waits, no other call is followed |
+| Voice channel | Tuned at 12.5 kHz like a conventional channel; RTCH frames are taken; every call there plays |
+| Back to control | Listening: no voice for 1 s (also the grace for the first frame), or 120 s on one call. The call just left is not followed again for 1.5 s. Talking: when our transmission ends |
+
+## 2. Channel numbers to frequencies
+
+SITE_INFO bits 88-111 hold the channel access information: bit 88 RCN, 89-90 step, 91-93 base.
+
+| RCN | Assignment field | Receive | Transmit |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| 1 (DFA) | bandwidth (2 bits, 62-63), OFN (16 bits, 64-79), IFN (16 bits, 80-95) | base + OFN x step | base + IFN x step |
+| 0, or no SITE_INFO yet | channel number (10 bits, 62-71) | plan base + number x plan spacing | receive + the control channel's duplex offset (its TX minus its RX) |
+
+DFA base 1: 100 MHz, 2: 330 MHz, 3: 400 MHz, 4: 750 MHz (DSD-FME; SDRTrunk reads 4 as 450 MHz: to confirm on air);
+step 2: 1.25 kHz, 3: 3.125 kHz. Other values, or channel numbers without a plan: not followed (and a voice request
+assigned there fails).
+
+## 3. Transmitting
+
+| Step | Behaviour |
+|---|---|
+| Registration | On the control channel, once SITE_INFO is heard (or after 2 s), REG_REQ goes out; no answer in 1 s: sent again, 3 tries, then a new round after 10 s, doubled up to 60 s. REG_RESP causes 0x01, 0x04, 0x05, 0x21, 0x24, 0x25 register; others are refused and retried the same way |
+| PTT | Refused until registered ("Release PTT, try again"). Registered: VCALL_REQ goes out (from a voice channel heard, back to control first); 3 tries 1 s apart. VCALL_RESP 0x10: wait for the assignment; 0x30-0x3F: queued, up to 10 s; others: refused |
+| Grant | VCALL_ASSGN with our unit as source and our target as destination: the receiver goes to the voice channel and the radio transmits on its inbound frequency (section 2), RTCH, inbound, with the channel's RAN, unit and target, and AES when the channel has it |
+| End | PTT released: the release frame, then back to the control channel. Released before the grant: the request is dropped |
+| Transmit allowed | The channel (its tag) allows transmitting, has a TX frequency, a unit ID and the board transmits the rate; otherwise the channel listens only |
+
+## 4. The codeplug and the radio
+
+The NXDN conventional block (nxdn-codeplug.md 2) grows by seven octets. A block of 5 octets or fewer is conventional.
+
+| Offset | Size | Field |
+|---|---|---|
+| 5 | 1 | Bit 0: trunked (the RX frequency is the control channel). Bit 1: follow every group (read only with bit 0). Bits 2-7: 0 |
+| 6 | 4 | Plan base, Hz, little endian; 0 = no plan |
+| 10 | 2 | Plan spacing in 125 Hz; 0 = no plan |
+
+The radio writes octets 0-5 when it saves a channel; the tag rewrite keeps 6-11 (the plan is the Codeplug Editor's).
+The contact gives the group followed and called (a private contact: an individual call, no group followed). The unit
+ID is the channel's or the radio's (nxdn-codeplug.md 1).
+
+| Where | What |
+|---|---|
+| Codeplug Editor, NXDN channel | Trunked, Follow every group, Channel plan base, Channel plan spacing |
+| Radio, channel editor | Row **Trunk** under Rate: Off, Group, All groups (a press steps through them). Saved with the channel |
+| Radio, VFO quick menu (NXDN) | Tile 9 **Trunk** in place of Bright: Off, Grp, All. The VFO's RX frequency is the control channel, its TX frequency the inbound one, DFA only (no plan), the TG tile's group. The value ends in `?` while not registered and `*` on a voice channel. Saving the VFO as a channel keeps it |
+
+## 5. Limits
+
+- One control channel; no adjacent-site or alternate control channel hunt, no roaming.
+- Bursts go out when the radio is ready, not aligned to the outbound frame timing; a site that needs aligned random
+  access may miss some (they are sent again).
+- No group registration (GRP_REG_REQ), registration clearing, authentication, emergency, data, status or
+  short-message services. Late entry into our own granted channel only through the assignment.
+- Channel-number sites need the linear plan; a site whose numbers are not linear needs a per-channel table (not
+  implemented). The VFO has no plan.
